<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ganghwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Japan–Korea Treaty of 1876</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Ganghwa was signed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in February 1876, after a Japanese naval clash near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a> in 1875. Modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> imposed on Japan, it opened Busan and later two other ports to Japanese trade, gave Japanese residents extraterritorial rights and declared Korea independent, undermining Chinese suzerainty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa.jpg" alt="Treaty of Ganghwa" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_Korea_Treaty_of_Amity_26_February_1876.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
